---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Elements" prose>
  <h1>Elements</h1>
  <p>
    <code>@hexpunk/core/elements.css</code> ships a token-driven baseline for native HTML
    primitives — paragraphs, links, lists, tables, code, form fields. Import it once at the
    document root and raw HTML in your app picks up the design system's typography, colour,
    focus ring, and forced-colors fallbacks. No <code>&lt;hp-*&gt;</code> wrapper required.
  </p>

  <hp-banner tone="neutral">
    Two layers in one file. The <strong>bare reset</strong> auto-applies to every native
    element. The <strong>.hp-prose</strong> class is an opt-in rhythm scope for long-form
    content. The flowing-text set — headings, inline marks, links, lists, blockquote,
    rules, and the prose rhythm — now lives on
    <a href="/components/typography/text">text &amp; prose</a>; tables, details, and the
    form baseline below move into their categories as those pages land.
  </hp-banner>

  <h2>Installation</h2>
  <hp-code language="html">{`<!-- Import the three CSS files once at your app's entry. -->
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/path/to/@hexpunk/core/tokens.dark.css" />
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/path/to/@hexpunk/core/tokens.light.css" />
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/path/to/@hexpunk/core/elements.css" />

<!-- Then write plain HTML — every native element picks up the
     design system's tokens automatically. -->
<p>Hello, hex world.</p>`}</hp-code>

  <p>
    Or, if your build pipeline supports CSS-via-JS imports (Vite, Astro, Next, etc.):
  </p>
  <hp-code language="javascript">{`import "@hexpunk/core/tokens.dark.css";
import "@hexpunk/core/tokens.light.css";
import "@hexpunk/core/elements.css";`}</hp-code>

  <h2>Tables</h2>
  <hp-demo caption="Caption, header, cells">
    <table>
      <caption>Density modes</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Mode</th>
          <th>Cell sm</th>
          <th>Cell md</th>
          <th>Cell lg</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>compact</td><td>44</td><td>80</td><td>140</td></tr>
        <tr><td>default</td><td>50</td><td>100</td><td>180</td></tr>
        <tr><td>comfortable</td><td>60</td><td>120</td><td>220</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <hp-code slot="code" language="html">{`<body>
  <table>
    <caption>Density modes</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Mode</th>
        <th>Cell sm</th>
        <th>Cell md</th>
        <th>Cell lg</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>compact</td><td>44</td><td>80</td><td>140</td></tr>
      <tr><td>default</td><td>50</td><td>100</td><td>180</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</body>`}</hp-code>
  </hp-demo>

  <h2>Details / summary</h2>
  <hp-demo caption="Native disclosure">
    <div>
      <details>
        <summary>Why hexagons?</summary>
        <p>
          Hexagons are the densest regular tiling of the plane — six neighbours per cell,
          uniform distance, and a directionality that maps cleanly to a six-state colour
          system. Every Hexpunk component starts from that geometry.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What's the difference between hp-cell and hp-hex?</summary>
        <p>
          <code>&lt;hp-hex&gt;</code> is the raw geometric primitive — pure shape, no
          semantics. <code>&lt;hp-cell&gt;</code> is the universal labelled atom built
          on top, with interactive variants (anchor / action / secondary / utility) and
          decorative variants (content / support / slot).
        </p>
      </details>
      <details open>
        <summary>Open by default</summary>
        <p>The <code>open</code> attribute starts the disclosure expanded.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
    <hp-code slot="code" language="html">{`<body>
  <details>
    <summary>Why hexagons?</summary>
    <p>Hexagons are the densest regular tiling of the plane...</p>
  </details>
  <details open>
    <summary>Open by default</summary>
    <p>The <code>open</code> attribute starts expanded.</p>
  </details>
</body>`}</hp-code>
  </hp-demo>

  <h2>Form primitives</h2>
  <hp-banner tone="neutral">
    Raw form inputs get tokenised typography, restrained borders, and a focus ring that
    matches the rest of the system. They deliberately don't carry hex geometry — for
    the full Hexpunk aesthetic (hex buttons, hex-bonded toggles, single/multi toggle
    groups, sliders, labelled selects, form-association via
    <a href="/components/forms/hp-form"><code>&lt;hp-form&gt;</code></a>), reach for:
    <a href="/components/forms/hp-button"><code>hp-button</code></a> ·
    <a href="/components/forms/hp-checkbox"><code>hp-checkbox</code></a> ·
    <a href="/components/forms/hp-radio"><code>hp-radio</code></a> ·
    <a href="/components/forms/hp-select"><code>hp-select</code></a> ·
    <a href="/components/forms/hp-slider"><code>hp-slider</code></a> ·
    <a href="/components/forms/hp-toggle"><code>hp-toggle</code></a> ·
    <a href="/components/forms/hp-toggle-group"><code>hp-toggle-group</code></a> ·
    <a href="/components/forms/hp-label"><code>hp-label</code></a>.
  </hp-banner>

  <hp-demo caption="Text inputs">
    <form
      onsubmit="event.preventDefault()"
      style="display: grid; gap: 12px; max-width: 32rem;"
    >
      <label>
        Name
        <input type="text" placeholder="Hissalia" style="display: block; width: 100%;">
      </label>
      <label>
        Email (validates on blur)
        <input type="email" required placeholder="izzy@example.com" style="display: block; width: 100%;">
      </label>
      <label>
        Bio
        <textarea placeholder="Tell us about your hex obsession" style="display: block; width: 100%;"></textarea>
      </label>
      <label>
        Country
        <select style="display: block; width: 100%;">
          <option>United Kingdom</option>
          <option>United States</option>
          <option>Japan</option>
        </select>
      </label>
    </form>
    <hp-code slot="code" language="html">{`<body>
  <form>
    <label>
      Name
      <input type="text" placeholder="Hissalia" />
    </label>
    <label>
      Email
      <input type="email" required placeholder="izzy@example.com" />
    </label>
    <label>
      Bio
      <textarea></textarea>
    </label>
    <label>
      Country
      <select>
        <option>United Kingdom</option>
        <option>United States</option>
      </select>
    </label>
  </form>
</body>`}</hp-code>
  </hp-demo>

  <hp-demo caption="Buttons, checkbox, radio, range">
    <div style="display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; align-items: center;">
      <button>raw button</button>
      <button disabled>disabled</button>
      <label><input type="checkbox" checked> Subscribe</label>
      <label><input type="radio" name="r" checked> A</label>
      <label><input type="radio" name="r"> B</label>
      <input type="range" min="0" max="100" value="42">
      <input type="color" value="#00cc88">
    </div>
    <hp-code slot="code" language="html">{`<body>
  <button>raw button</button>
  <button disabled>disabled</button>
  <label><input type="checkbox" checked /> Subscribe</label>
  <label><input type="radio" name="r" /> A</label>
  <input type="range" min="0" max="100" value="42" />
  <input type="color" value="#00cc88" />
</body>`}</hp-code>
  </hp-demo>

  <hp-demo caption="Fieldset + legend">
    <fieldset style="max-width: 32rem;">
      <legend>Display preferences</legend>
      <label style="display: block;"><input type="checkbox"> Reduce motion</label>
      <label style="display: block;"><input type="checkbox" checked> Show focus rings</label>
      <label style="display: block;"><input type="checkbox"> High contrast</label>
    </fieldset>
    <hp-code slot="code" language="html">{`<body>
  <fieldset>
    <legend>Display preferences</legend>
    <label><input type="checkbox" /> Reduce motion</label>
    <label><input type="checkbox" checked /> Show focus rings</label>
    <label><input type="checkbox" /> High contrast</label>
  </fieldset>
</body>`}</hp-code>
  </hp-demo>

  <h2>What this file does NOT do</h2>
  <ul class="hp-prose">
    <li>Restyle <code>&lt;hp-*&gt;</code> elements — they live in shadow DOM and own their own chrome.</li>
    <li>Repaint <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> / <code>&lt;input&gt;</code> as full hp-button / hp-input lookalikes — hex geometry stays reserved for the components.</li>
    <li>Force a max-width or font-size root override — the page shell is yours.</li>
  </ul>
</Layout>
